1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:09,890
Alright, next section that we want to talk about is the ovary, and the way I've handled ovary in the text is the first section in your text actually deals with non-neoplastic ovary.

2
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:17,930
The second part of the text dealing with ovary deals with neoplastic ovary, and I'm going to actually do these in two separate sections.

3
00:00:18,550 --> 00:00:22,210
Now remember, before we can even understand the ovary, we've got to understand the normal.

4
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:25,310
The functional unit of the ovary is called the follicle.

5
00:00:25,430 --> 00:00:29,290
There are multiple follicles within the ovary, but that is the functional unit.

6
00:00:29,290 --> 00:00:31,910
The follicle basically consists of three cells.

7
00:00:32,070 --> 00:00:41,650
It consists of an oocyte, which is the egg, and then surrounding that oocyte are the granulosa cells, and then outside of the granulosa cells are the theca cells.

8
00:00:42,410 --> 00:00:51,090
Alright, and it's important to remember from normal physiology that recall that what hits the theca cells, it's called the luteinizing hormone.

9
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:57,450
Luteinizing hormone hits the theca cells, which then induces production of androgen by the theca cells.

10
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:16,590
At the same time, follicle stimulating hormone hits the granulosa cells, which then allows the granulosa cells to take that androgen and to convert it to estrogen, estradiol, and then that estradiol goes and bathes the egg, and that allows for the egg to mature or the oocyte to mature.

11
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:23,230
I'm going to repeat that again because it's very high yield to understand these basics for what I'm going to talk about as we move forward.

12
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:27,670
Luteinizing hormone from the pituitary hits the theca cell.

13
00:01:28,070 --> 00:01:30,590
The theca cell then converts, then produces androgen.

14
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:33,990
Androgen then goes to the granulosa cell.

15
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:38,590
Under the influence of FSH, the granulosa cell converts the androgen to estradiol.

16
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:44,150
Estradiol then hits the egg, and when it hits the egg, it allows for the egg to mature.

17
00:01:44,150 --> 00:01:45,830
So this is the normal.

18
00:01:46,190 --> 00:01:47,870
I'm going to show you this histologically.

19
00:01:48,270 --> 00:01:49,650
Here we have the egg.

20
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:58,070
This represents the granulosa cells, and this represents the fecal cells.

21
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:02,390
Again, the fecal cells under the influence of LH will produce androgen.

22
00:02:02,710 --> 00:02:06,990
The granulosa cells under the influence of FSH will convert that androgen to estrogen.

23
00:02:07,470 --> 00:02:08,390
So that's the normal.

24
00:02:08,390 --> 00:02:19,310
Now, once you get the bathing of the egg with estrogen, then eventually what's going to happen is that estrogen is going to result in the proliferative phase within the endometrium as well.

25
00:02:19,650 --> 00:02:22,770
So not only is the egg maturing, but the estrogen goes out into the blood.

26
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:26,590
It hits the endometrium, causing the endometrium to undergo proliferative phase.

27
00:02:26,590 --> 00:02:34,830
Eventually, there will be ovulation of this egg, and once there's ovulation of the egg, we now go into the secretory phase of the endometrial cycle,

28
00:02:35,310 --> 00:02:43,430
and the residual cells, both the granulosa cells, theca cells, this residual area, will begin to produce primarily progesterone.

29
00:02:43,430 --> 00:02:51,290
And remember that progesterone hits the endometrium and prepares the endometrium via the secretory phase for implantation,

30
00:02:51,490 --> 00:02:56,270
which obviously these cells are hoping will occur because they work so hard to produce this beautiful egg.

31
00:02:56,850 --> 00:03:03,170
So after ovulation, the residual follicle now becomes called a corpus luteum.

32
00:03:03,690 --> 00:03:11,030
The corpus luteum primarily secretes progesterone, which is going to prepare the endometrium for the pregnancy.

33
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:18,890
It is possible when you have a corpus luteum to get bleeding into the corpus luteum, and that would be called a hemorrhagic luteal cyst.

34
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:23,350
And that's one way by which you can have a, quote-unquote, mass within the ovary,

35
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:29,990
but it would actually just not really be a mass, but just a problem of bleeding into a corpus luteum.

36
00:03:30,430 --> 00:03:32,010
Here's what the corpus luteum looks like.

37
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:34,070
I just want to show you how big it becomes.

38
00:03:34,410 --> 00:03:38,850
Basically, this is the ovary, and you can clearly see that this is the corpus luteum here.

39
00:03:38,850 --> 00:03:43,630
First of all, it's got this yellow color to it because it's producing hormones,

40
00:03:43,970 --> 00:03:46,110
and so that's one important thing to bear in mind.

41
00:03:46,550 --> 00:03:49,250
Another important thing to recognize is how big it is.

42
00:03:49,290 --> 00:03:53,890
It's really almost the size of the ovary, and that's because it really is the functional unit of the ovary,

43
00:03:53,950 --> 00:03:57,730
and once it gets going and its egg is selected for ovulation,

44
00:03:58,230 --> 00:04:01,550
then it really takes over the ovary and drives what the ovary does.

45
00:04:01,990 --> 00:04:04,250
So that's the corpus luteum.

46
00:04:04,250 --> 00:04:08,070
Of course, I've told you that you can actually result, you can get bleeding into the corpus luteum,

47
00:04:08,110 --> 00:04:10,690
which would then expand it further and make it look like a mass,

48
00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:13,270
and that's called a hemorrhagic luteal cyst.

49
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:17,470
Now, the follicles within the ovary, they can degenerate.

50
00:04:17,610 --> 00:04:22,430
There are so many follicles within the ovary, and it's possible that one or two of those follicles can degenerate.

51
00:04:22,490 --> 00:04:26,390
So imagine if this is the ovary, and I've got all these little circular follicles here.

52
00:04:26,390 --> 00:04:31,570
If one degenerates, what happens when they degenerate is they become big and cystic,

53
00:04:31,990 --> 00:04:35,090
so that when you have a degenerated follicle, it becomes big and cystic,

54
00:04:35,150 --> 00:04:44,430
and most women will have one or two or three follicular cysts, i.e. degenerated follicular cysts, within their ovary.

55
00:04:44,870 --> 00:04:49,530
There is a disorder, however, in which patients can have multiple follicular cysts,

56
00:04:49,530 --> 00:04:55,090
and that's called polycystic ovarian disease, and of course, that is due to a hormone imbalance,

57
00:04:55,090 --> 00:04:57,950
and so we need to understand this disorder a little further.

58
00:04:59,010 --> 00:05:02,450
This disorder is characterized by an increased luteinizing hormone.

59
00:05:03,650 --> 00:05:06,050
That's the way to start when you think about this disorder,

60
00:05:06,410 --> 00:05:10,450
and because there's an increased luteinizing hormone, eventually there's going to be a low FSH,

61
00:05:11,010 --> 00:05:17,990
such that the key characteristic in this disease will be that the LH to FSH ratio will be greater than two.

62
00:05:18,610 --> 00:05:19,590
Now, let me take a step back.

63
00:05:19,590 --> 00:05:24,670
This is a very complicated disorder when you're studying this from the perspective of endocrinology.

64
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:29,510
And there's a lot of people who have their explanations out there about exactly what's going on.

65
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:30,890
I'm going to give you one.

66
00:05:31,110 --> 00:05:35,650
You can just take it with a grain of salt, but I think it'll help you to remember what's going on

67
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:40,950
and sort of try to create a paradigm in your mind in a way that you can remember it.

68
00:05:41,010 --> 00:05:42,530
So let's start this way.

69
00:05:42,930 --> 00:05:46,910
Let's remind ourselves that in this disease, the key problem is an increased LH, right?

70
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:54,070
So here's the egg, here are the granulosa cells, and here are the, sorry, these are the fecal cells, all right?

71
00:05:54,530 --> 00:05:56,630
Now, the increased LH is going to do what?

72
00:05:56,690 --> 00:06:02,070
It's going to hit the fecal cell, and in response to the increased LH, the fecal cells are going to produce androgen.

73
00:06:02,070 --> 00:06:07,370
All right, so there's going to be a high amount of androgen because there's a high amount of LH.

74
00:06:07,750 --> 00:06:11,610
Now, what that androgen will do is it will go into the blood, and when it goes into the blood,

75
00:06:11,930 --> 00:06:15,310
the androgen will create hertstuism within the patient.

76
00:06:15,410 --> 00:06:16,150
What is hertstuism?

77
00:06:16,270 --> 00:06:18,930
It is excess hair in a male distribution.

78
00:06:19,510 --> 00:06:23,370
So the patients may begin to develop heavy facial hair, for example.

79
00:06:24,030 --> 00:06:28,210
Now, at the same time, some of that androgen will actually go into the peripheral adipose tissue,

80
00:06:28,210 --> 00:06:32,510
and in the peripheral adipose tissue, the androgen will be converted to estrone.

81
00:06:34,330 --> 00:06:37,330
Estrone is the estrogen made by adipose tissue.

82
00:06:37,630 --> 00:06:41,150
Remember that you get peripheral conversion of androgen to estrogen,

83
00:06:41,450 --> 00:06:43,470
and that type of estrogen is called estrone.

84
00:06:44,330 --> 00:06:49,410
Now, these patients tend to be obese, so they tend to produce large amounts of estrone as well.

85
00:06:49,790 --> 00:06:55,390
That estrone will go back to the pituitary and will feed back and shut down production of FSH.

86
00:06:55,390 --> 00:07:03,910
If there's decreased production of FSH, these granulosa cells are going to not be able to do their job.

87
00:07:04,410 --> 00:07:07,390
They're not going to be able to convert androgen to estradiol,

88
00:07:07,550 --> 00:07:10,130
which was the job of the granulosa cells, if you'll recall.

89
00:07:10,730 --> 00:07:15,730
And so what's going to happen in these patients is that they're then subsequently going to get

90
00:07:15,730 --> 00:07:18,510
a degeneration of this follicle,

91
00:07:18,630 --> 00:07:23,230
because they're not able to produce the estrogen necessary to be able to maintain the follicle.

92
00:07:23,230 --> 00:07:26,250
So the follicle will degenerate and become cystic.

93
00:07:26,670 --> 00:07:32,270
And that's what results in the polycystic ovarian classic picture,

94
00:07:32,470 --> 00:07:36,730
the follicular degeneration with multiple cysts within the ovary.

95
00:07:37,090 --> 00:07:38,570
So what is the classic presentation?

96
00:07:38,950 --> 00:07:42,090
You have an obese woman, that makes sense given what I've just talked about,

97
00:07:42,370 --> 00:07:44,790
who has infertility because she's not ovulating.

98
00:07:45,290 --> 00:07:48,130
She has oligomenorrhea because she's not going through,

99
00:07:48,130 --> 00:07:52,890
the entire cycle is messed up because the hormone balance is messed up,

100
00:07:53,090 --> 00:07:54,450
and she's got her Stuart's features,

101
00:07:54,570 --> 00:07:58,130
which means that she's got hair in a male distribution pattern.

102
00:07:58,770 --> 00:08:02,830
Now, that's the classic presentation, and it all ties into what I've already explained.

103
00:08:03,310 --> 00:08:07,190
What I'd also like to remind you of is that a subset of these patients have insulin resistance.

104
00:08:07,670 --> 00:08:09,390
So that 10 to 15 years down the line,

105
00:08:09,490 --> 00:08:14,070
a subset of these patients will actually develop type 2 diabetes mellitus,

106
00:08:14,070 --> 00:08:15,890
and that is high-yield association.

107
00:08:16,430 --> 00:08:19,630
And then another high-yield association is that these patients,

108
00:08:19,790 --> 00:08:22,010
because they have high circulating levels of estrone,

109
00:08:23,010 --> 00:08:25,410
will have an increased risk for endometrial carcinoma.

110
00:08:25,870 --> 00:08:29,570
And recall that endometrial carcinoma, the key risk factor, was estrogen.

111
00:08:29,750 --> 00:08:33,230
So the more estrogen that a patient sees across their lifetime,

112
00:08:33,550 --> 00:08:36,310
the more likely they are to develop endometrial carcinoma.

113
00:08:36,670 --> 00:08:38,890
So the high circulating estrone levels,

114
00:08:39,010 --> 00:08:41,570
because the patient's obese and has large amounts of androgen,

115
00:08:41,570 --> 00:08:43,570
and the androgen gets converted to estrone again,

116
00:08:43,570 --> 00:08:50,730
that high circulating levels of estrone will increase the risk for endometrial carcinoma.

117
00:08:51,230 --> 00:08:57,670
And so that's the key disorder that I want to talk about concerning the non-neoplastic ovary.

118
00:08:58,130 --> 00:09:02,990
And that is also going to be useful for our discussion of ovarian tumors,

119
00:09:03,550 --> 00:09:06,410
but you'll see what I mean when we go to the next section.

120
00:09:06,730 --> 00:09:09,150
So we'll stop there, and then we'll continue with ovarian tumors.

